# Feature-Sliced Design in this project

> Generated by `sveltekit-fsd init`. Edit freely — this file is the convention,
> not a lock file. Keep it in step with the code; a doc describing the previous
> shape is worse than no doc.

## Layout

```text
{{srcDir}}/
├── app/                     # FSD app layer — and SvelteKit's own entry points
│   ├── index.html           # appTemplate (was {{srcDir}}/app.html)
│   ├── routes/              # SvelteKit routing — thin, see below
│   ├── layouts/             # shared route shells
│   ├── providers/           # context providers, mounted once in +layout.svelte
│   └── styles/              # the project-wide stylesheet and theme
├── pages/<page>/            # one slice per route: ui/, index.ts (public API)
├── features/<slice>/        # a whole user action, once two pages need it
├── entities/<slice>/        # a business object, once two features need it
└── shared/                  # infrastructure only — api/, auth/, config/, lib/, ui/
```

Routing lives **inside** the app layer, which is what the
[FSD SvelteKit guide](https://feature-sliced.design/docs/guides/tech/with-sveltekit)
asks for and the opposite of where SvelteKit puts it by default. These settings
make that real, and they live in the SvelteKit config (`vite.config.ts` in a
project made by a recent `sv create`, `svelte.config.js` in an older one):

```js
files: {
  routes: '{{routesDir}}',
  appTemplate: '{{srcDir}}/app/index.html'
},
alias: {
  '{{alias}}/*': '{{srcDir}}/*'
}
```

`kit.files.lib` is **not** set, although the FSD guide sets it to `'{{srcDir}}'`.
Pointing `$lib` at the layers would mean moving everything already in
`{{srcDir}}/lib/` and rewriting every `$lib/` import, and `init` moves no code
anyone wrote. So `$lib` still means `{{srcDir}}/lib/` — SvelteKit's own
directory, not a layer. Shared code goes in `shared/` and is imported as
`{{alias}}/shared/...`; that is the only spelling the import boundary knows, and
steiger ignores `{{srcDir}}/lib/`. Do not grow it: two homes for shared code is
the drift the layers exist to prevent.

`init` does not touch `tsconfig.json`: SvelteKit writes the alias into the
generated `.svelte-kit/tsconfig.json` that yours extends, so `svelte-check`,
your editor and steiger all resolve `{{alias}}/` from the same place Vite does.
`static/` is left where SvelteKit put it — the FSD guide renames it to `public/`,
which is a rename with no FSD content, and a rename is not free.

**Add layers when a second consumer actually appears, not up front.**
Duplicating a component across two pages is cheaper to undo than an `entities/`
layer built for a reuse that never happens.

`widgets/` is left closed here, and that is **this project's** call rather than
the spec's. FSD v2.1 has it as an ordinary layer; its only caution is that a UI
block which is most of a page's content and is never reused should not be one.
The reasoning for closing it: a reusable block with its own fetching and state
is already a feature, so the widget/feature boundary would be one more decision
per screen buying nothing. Reach for `features/` first. Reopen it by deleting
this paragraph, not by asking the spec's permission.

When reuse becomes real, choose the owner by meaning:

- a generic client, transport wrapper, or authentication plumbing →
  `shared/api` or `shared/auth`
- a reusable business resource and its entity-related requests →
  `entities/<name>/api` and `entities/<name>/model`
- a reusable user action → `features/<name>/api` and `features/<name>/model`
- a request used by one page → `pages/<name>/api`

Generic CRUD primitives can stay in `shared/api`. A domain-specific request
should not be put in `shared/<domain>` just to avoid creating an entity; if its
owner is still unclear, keep it in the consuming page until the boundary is
stable.

**Two signals say that code should move below the consuming page.** Neither is
a page count — a shared API client read by three pages can be perfectly correct.

The first is a **business rule landing in `shared/`**. Shared is the layer with
no business meaning, so move that rule to the owning entity, feature, page, or
app composition.

The second is **two `pages` slices each writing their own copy** of the same API
resource. They cannot import each other, so nothing makes the two agree — not
review, not the compiler. Move the request to the owning entity or feature, or
keep a genuinely generic CRUD primitive in `shared/api`.

The full methodology is in the `feature-sliced-design` skill `init` wrote at
`.agents/skills/feature-sliced-design/` — layer definitions, the `@x`
cross-import pattern, when an entity earns its keep, and asset placement. This
file is only how it lands in *this* repo.

